Date: Tue, 18 Jan 94 22:04:27 EST
From: Tim Freeman <tsf@cs.cmu.edu>
Subject: net tech; FTP via NFS

If you can mount NFS volumes on your Mac (or other machine), then you
can make the world's anonymous FTP servers look like an
NFS file system.  Just set up or find an Alex server, and mount the
NFS volume it exports.  The server caches, so it's faster after the first
time you access a file.  

For more information about Alex, use anonymous FTP to fetch the file
alex/README from alex.sp.cs.cmu.edu.  For instance, the file I just
mentioned is available to me as

    /alex/edu/cmu/cs/sp/alex/alex/README

which I can visit from Emacs like any other file.  I can also use
commands that manipulate directories.  On a unix machine, this
happens:

% ls /alex/edu/cmu/cs/sp/alex/alex
README		demo		ls-lR		tools
alex		doc		readmes		www
cs-techreports	links		src
% 

I can use find, tar, unzip, zcat, and so forth on the Alex volume like
any other.

My friend Vince who made Alex actually subscribes to this list, but I
doubt he takes the time to read much of it.

Tim

