Date: Tue, 18 Jan 1994 23:17:36 -0100
From: carlf@media.mit.edu (Carl Feynman)
Subject: HST fixed (we hope)

Amara Graps says:
>Steve Witham asked about observing the whole sky with the Hubble
>Telescope (how long would it take and how much data would be generated).

I didn't see the message that started this thread, so I'm not sure that I'm
answering the right question.  But starting from the data contained in
Amara's message, I can make some back-of-the-envelope estimates.

The resolution of the telescope is 2.5e-7 radians.  If we wanted to take a
picture of the whole sky with pixels this big, it would take 4 pi
(2.5e-7)^2 = 8e15 pixels.  If we used a paltry sixteen bits per pixel, this
would be 16,000 terabytes, and would take 22,000 years to transmit to the
ground at 2 gbytes/day.  Note that the Hubble is probably capable of
getting a lot more than 16 bits per pixel, given its spectrometers and
suchlike.

There's a lot of sky out there.


