From: fnerd@smds.com (FutureNerd Steve Witham)
Subject: Emotional involvement

Tim May says on another thread-

> As to some comments Kennita made last night about the neat things we
> do--software, crypto, etc.--needing to have a human face, that we not
> just thinkers but also feelers (apologies if I have altered her
> intended meaning with this summar....), I strongly disagree that these
> things are being done or worked on in an unemotional way. Anyone who
> has seen me talk excitedly about cryto (and the Cypherpunks may not be
> the best venue for seeing this, for schedule reasons), or about
> nanotech, knows this.
> 
> My emotional attachment, what motivates me, is clearly visible. In
> fact, I wrote up some posts on exactly this topic, the adrenalin rush
> of discovering something nobody in the know universe knows except
> oneself (or one's self). I described this incredible rush in some
> posts responding to Amara's points about women in science and noted
> that this rush is what drives me forward.

Let me make an example of myself.  I would love to work on AI and 
artificial evolution.  I think up ideas in these fields all the time.  
I'm proud of my ideas; I get excited; I get absorbed.

But I don't want to hasten the obsolescence of biology, the
retrofitting of culture, and the scan-capture of nature.

On the other hand, I think the simplification and clarification of
software, the whole software creation and use process, is an
essential step towards a world where people are more in charge of 
their lives and aware and skeptical about the designs of others. 
I have a couple of good ideas about it.  But not very often.

If anything, putting a human face on technology isn't a radical enough
goal.  For instance, simplicity (as Ted Nelson says) can't be added.

I feel that something should be done about it.  But somehow I'm not
quite excited enough to do it.

I know that in Tim's case the goals and the technical excitement
line up pretty much.  But for some of us there is a problem.

There is no implication here that we don't feel.  The problem is that
feelings lead to actions that have effects that lead to feelings that
don't necessarily match the originals.  I don't find it a simple 
problem.

-fnerd
