From: vincek@netcom.com (Vince Kerchner)
Subject: Extropian love

Nick Szabo makes some really excellent points in his recent post on
this thread:

>Rational theories of human relationships need to be falsified in terms
>of human emotions, aesthetics, instincts, etc. just as rational
>theories of other aspects of nature need to be falsified against the
>concrete reality observed in those phenomenon.

I agree completely.  But what are the axioms and productions of such a
rational system of human relationships from which propositions may be
formed and by which they may be tested?  I think that if we could
answer this question constructively, we could revolutionize cognitive
psychology and therapy.  I don't mean to simply be "touchy-feely"
here, I mean to suggest that the successful codification of human
emotions, behaviors, and dysfunctions could significantly affect the
lives of everyone on the planet.  This sounds like serious
"self-transformation" to me.

>It's possible that good theories of relationships are so logically
>deep that:
>* They cannot be expressed or understand by our human brains without
>very lossy compression, which probably takes the form of metaphorical
>reference to similarly complex phenomenon;

I think this is true as well, but why?  Furthermore, I don't think
this is substantially different from what takes place in other areas
of human experience, including science.  Many theories are logically
deep, so much so that typically only their conclusions or paradigms
(in the form of metaphorical systems) are presented to an audience,
especially during initial contact.  Basic, primary sources of
information are often presented only as part of advanced, evolved
transactions once the tokens of the representation have been
assimilated, if ever.

>* It's computationally infeasible to create such a theory over a
>single (unaugmented) human lifetime;

However, what about an ensemble of unaugmented lifetimes, with serial
communication and information storage being provided between
individuals?   Perhaps this is ultimately the source of kin or clan
behavioral dynamics, or even of the family unit itself: the need to
artificially augment the information storage and processing power of a
single individual, and to distribute the risk of information loss due
to the finite lifespan of a single individual.

>If so, the very compressed theories of previous generations provide
>vastly more sophisticated and useful guide for relationships than
>any we could generate ourselves, from scratch.  Theories compressed
> [etc]
>This might require "memetic archaeology"

Excellent!  "Memetic archaeology" exists, and is called mythology.
This might be the very reason why for all of conscious human
existence, and far pre-dating "literate" civilization, we have been so
interested in myths, legends, songs, and storytelling.  Not for
entertainment, but as a vast database of compiled human experience
which transcends death, which is used to guide new generations, and
which provides comparative filters for assessing new behaviors,
relationships, and boundaries.  Many authors, including Joseph
Campbell and Clarissa Estes, speak of archetypal myths and stories
which are common to almost all cultures, and which are used to
represent and test rites of passage and other dynamic psychological
processes. 

>On the other hand it might be extremely entropic to use theories
>created from scratch with our current very limited brains instead of
>using the vast, logically deep storehouse of lessons that billions of
>humans have already learned.

Unfortunately, no human "racial memory" exists, and it is a sad fact
of evolution and genetics that experiences of an individual are not
passed on to new generations without explicit training.  However, if
we were to develop an appropriate retrovirus, perhaps there is enough
redundancy in the human genotype to encode this kind of information.


